WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:08.510
<v Speaker 1>You are stepping into the study of a weary student, opening a text titled, O Beloved Son, which carries a stark and uncompromising teaching.

2
00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:14.590
<v Speaker 1>The knowledge a scholar never acts on may be the exact thing for which he is most severely judged.

3
00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:18.990
<v Speaker 2>Knowledge alone without righteous deeds will not benefit you on the day of judgment.

4
00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:26.350
<v Speaker 1>And we really need to sit with the context here because we are examining a highly specific crisis of faith and intellect.

5
00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:26.990
<v Speaker 2>Right.

6
00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:30.590
<v Speaker 2>The subject is a disciple who is absolutely not a beginner.

7
00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:37.390
<v Speaker 2>He has spent years accumulating study, gathering texts, memorizing vast theoretical concepts.

8
00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:42.670
<v Speaker 1>He's been absorbing the wisdom of his teacher for years, yet he writes to his teacher asking for something distinct.

9
00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:44.990
<v Speaker 1>He asks for saving knowledge.

10
00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:46.670
<v Speaker 2>Which is a heavy phrase, you know.

11
00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:53.150
<v Speaker 2>And to frame this request, he invokes a specific prayer from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family.

12
00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:56.270
<v Speaker 1>We should look at the exact words he uses to frame that anxiety.

13
00:00:56.350 --> 00:01:00.830
<v Speaker 2>The prayer is, I seek Allah's refuge from knowledge that is of no benefit.

14
00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:03.710
<v Speaker 1>We need to pause and examine the psychology of that request.

15
00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:07.710
<v Speaker 1>Seeking refuge implies an active, looming threat.

16
00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:08.510
<v Speaker 2>It does.

17
00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:15.310
<v Speaker 2>I mean, we are conditioned to view education and information as inherently good or at worst completely neutral.

18
00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:20.270
<v Speaker 1>The student's use of this prayer reveals a deep internal anxiety.

19
00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:27.630
<v Speaker 1>He possesses vast amounts of information, but he suspects deep down that much of it will not help him when it actually counts.

20
00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:32.030
<v Speaker 2>He is asking the master who wrote this letter to his student, Gaz A.H.

21
00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:35.230
<v Speaker 2>Lee, to sift through everything he has ever been taught.

22
00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:40.270
<v Speaker 2>He wants him to hand over the single distilled piece of knowledge that will spare him.

23
00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:43.310
<v Speaker 1>That anxiety is the engine of the entire text.

24
00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:50.430
<v Speaker 1>To treat knowledge as something from which you must seek divine protection, it completely inverts how we usually think about learning.

25
00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:54.670
<v Speaker 2>We operate under the assumption that acquiring more information is always a net positive.

26
00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:55.670
<v Speaker 2>We hoard facts.

27
00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:57.790
<v Speaker 1>We build these massive internal libraries.

28
00:01:57.790 --> 00:01:58.350
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

29
00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:01.310
<v Speaker 2>But the prophet's prayer shatters that assumption entirely.

30
00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:06.990
<v Speaker 2>It establishes A category of knowledge that is not merely useless, but actively dangerous to the soul.

31
00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:08.990
<v Speaker 1>The student senses this danger.

32
00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:11.630
<v Speaker 1>He feels the weight of his unacted upon learning.

33
00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:13.830
<v Speaker 2>But his proposed solution is fundamentally

34
00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:14.350
<v Speaker 2>flawed.

35
00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:19.070
<v Speaker 2>He assumes the cure for his anxiety is to acquire yet another teaching.

36
00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:23.550
<v Speaker 1>Like a secret summary or a final masterful distillation from his teacher.

37
00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:29.390
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, he wants one more concept to memorize, hoping that the right concept will finally spark the transformation.

38
00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:33.150
<v Speaker 1>The master's response refuses this premise completely.

39
00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:38.990
<v Speaker 1>It is an immediate heavy blow that dismantles the student's entire framework of seeking.

40
00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:47.230
<v Speaker 2>The letter opens with conventional warmth, praises, blessings, addressing the student as his dear beloved son and true friend.

41
00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:51.230
<v Speaker 1>That warmth only serves to heighten the impact of what immediately follows.

42
00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:54.590
<v Speaker 1>The master turns the entire premise of the letter back on the disciple.

43
00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:59.550
<v Speaker 2>He points to the outward conduct and inner states of the prophet and the traditions that carry that counsel.

44
00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:05.870
<v Speaker 1>He tells the student plainly, if you have received guidance from those sources, you need no further counsel from me.

45
00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:09.230
<v Speaker 2>And if you have not received benefit from them, then you must ask yourself

46
00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:11.710
<v Speaker 2>you have truly been doing all these years.

47
00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:15.070
<v Speaker 2>He is holding up a mirror to the student's paralysis.

48
00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:24.430
<v Speaker 1>The master refuses to hand over a new piece of information because he diagnoses the problem not as a lack of water, but as a flaw in the vessel.

49
00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:27.230
<v Speaker 2>The fountain of guidance has been pouring for years.

50
00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:29.790
<v Speaker 2>The water is abundant, pure, and accessible.

51
00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:37.870
<v Speaker 1>If you find yourself still thirsty after years of standing under that fountain, the master suggests the problem is not that you are standing under the wrong source.

52
00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:41.070
<v Speaker 2>The problem is that your cup is turned upside down.

53
00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:44.750
<v Speaker 2>The student's letter is essentially asking, which is the right water.

54
00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:47.390
<v Speaker 1>The master's response redirects the inquiry.

55
00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:49.710
<v Speaker 1>Why is the cup turned upside down?

56
00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:52.270
<v Speaker 2>The deficit is never on the side of the teaching.

57
00:03:52.510 --> 00:04:00.270
<v Speaker 2>The deficit is entirely in the posture of the student who diligently gathers the water, studies its properties, but never actually drinks it.

58
00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:06.350
<v Speaker 1>Keeping the cup turned upside down functions as a brilliant, deceptive protective mechanism for you.

59
00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:11.630
<v Speaker 1>It allows a person to stand near the water, to converse endlessly about the water.

60
00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:20.030
<v Speaker 2>And to be seen by others as a dedicated seeker of the water, all without ever having to undergo the painful internal transformation that comes from actually swallowing it.

61
00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:26.110
<v Speaker 1>The student feels safe because he is in the vicinity of truth, confusing proximity for consumption.

62
00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:30.910
<v Speaker 2>That distinction between proximity and consumption brings us to the diagnostic lens of the text.

63
00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:35.230
<v Speaker 2>The master stacks 3 severe warnings at the very head of the letter.

64
00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:37.390
<v Speaker 1>These are not general pieces of advice.

65
00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:41.870
<v Speaker 1>They are diagnostic criteria through which you must evaluate your own life.

66
00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:45.310
<v Speaker 2>The first warning addresses how a life is spent.

67
00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:52.670
<v Speaker 2>It states, a servant's preoccupation with useless matters is a sign that Allah has withdrawn his special favor from him.

68
00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.710
<v Speaker 1>That is a staggering metric to apply to your daily routine.

69
00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:56.750
<v Speaker 2>It goes further.

70
00:04:56.750 --> 00:05:06.590
<v Speaker 2>He adds, and if even a single moment of a servant's life passes in pursuit of something other than that for which Allah created him, then that is a matter of immense sorrow.

71
00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:08.830
<v Speaker 1>Look at the twofold nature of this verdict.

72
00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:09.630
<v Speaker 1>First,

73
00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:11.790
<v Speaker 1>there is the tragedy of wasted time.

74
00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:15.710
<v Speaker 2>Second and infinitely more concerning is the theological implication.

75
00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:20.350
<v Speaker 2>The waste is not treated as a mere accident of scheduling or a lapse in focus.

76
00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:25.150
<v Speaker 1>It is identified as the visible mark of an invisible withdrawal of divine favor.

77
00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:33.470
<v Speaker 1>But defining any preoccupation with useless matters as a sign of divine withdrawal leaves literally no room for human exhaustion, does it?

78
00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:39.230
<v Speaker 1>Are we demanding A relentless machine-like productivity from a fragile human soul?

79
00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:44.430
<v Speaker 1>where even a brief rest or an aimless hour signifies that God has abandoned us.

80
00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:49.230
<v Speaker 2>I will leave a moment of complete silence right here to honor the weight of that challenge.

81
00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:55.790
<v Speaker 2>The text is addressing the overarching trajectory of a life, not the necessary mechanics of physical recovery.

82
00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:58.590
<v Speaker 1>So it's not a blanket condemnation of sleeping or eating.

83
00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:01.310
<v Speaker 2>No, the Master is not speaking against rest.

84
00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:06.830
<v Speaker 2>Rest that sustains the body and mind so that the individual can return to their purpose is not a useless matter.

85
00:06:07.070 --> 00:06:09.870
<v Speaker 2>It is the required maintenance of the human vessel.

86
00:06:09.950 --> 00:06:14.190
<v Speaker 1>What this warning targets is the active, habitual pursuit of the irrelevant.

87
00:06:14.350 --> 00:06:24.190
<v Speaker 2>It specifically addresses the scholar or the seeker who spends their waking hours debating trivialities, chasing status, or losing themselves in pursuits that carry zero weight in the ultimate accounting.

88
00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:27.630
<v Speaker 1>When a life becomes patterned by these endless empty pursuits,

89
00:06:27.950 --> 00:06:31.150
<v Speaker 1>It indicates that the soul has been left to its own devices.

90
00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:35.230
<v Speaker 2>The withdrawal of divine favor is entirely invisible from the inside.

91
00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:37.390
<v Speaker 2>You do not feel a sudden absence.

92
00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:43.470
<v Speaker 1>The only way you can detect that you have been left alone is by looking objectively at what fills your hours.

93
00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:51.390
<v Speaker 2>If your hours are consumed by matters that evaporate upon your death, that emptiness is the primary diagnostic symptom that favor has been withdrawn.

94
00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:54.750
<v Speaker 1>Unacted upon knowledge creates a habit of spiritual drift.

95
00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:57.830
<v Speaker 2>And that drift is the evidence of the withdrawal.

96
00:06:57.910 --> 00:07:05.030
<v Speaker 1>Critically at those hours leads directly into the master's second warning, which imposes A rigid, inescapable timeline on you.

97
00:07:05.390 --> 00:07:14.590
<v Speaker 2>The warning states, an individual whose condition, even after 40 years of age, is such that the good within him does not prevail over the evil should prepare to go to hell.

98
00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:17.230
<v Speaker 1>It completely removes the illusion of endless tomorrows.

99
00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:22.110
<v Speaker 2>The 40-year clock is a structural, biological, and spiritual reality in this teaching.

100
00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:28.910
<v Speaker 2>The master acknowledges that giving this advice is simple, but absorbing its reality is agonizing.

101
00:07:29.070 --> 00:07:31.470
<v Speaker 1>Consider what actually happens at the age of 40.

102
00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:37.870
<v Speaker 2>The period of youth, of endless formation, of exploring limitless potential permanently closes.

103
00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:41.790
<v Speaker 1>The physical structure of the human being reaches its absolute peak.

104
00:07:42.310 --> 00:07:44.670
<v Speaker 1>and begins its slow, inevitable descent.

105
00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:50.430
<v Speaker 2>The warning suggests that if, by the time that physical pick is reached, the inner scales have not tipped.

106
00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:55.150
<v Speaker 1>If the trajectory of the individual's habits and actions is not yet net upward.

107
00:07:55.230 --> 00:07:58.270
<v Speaker 2>Then the time for unconcerned drift has expired.

108
00:07:58.430 --> 00:08:00.590
<v Speaker 2>The in reality has calcified.

109
00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:05.710
<v Speaker 1>Applying this 40-year clock to you forces a harsh confrontation with your own inner scale.

110
00:08:06.310 --> 00:08:10.190
<v Speaker 1>We operate under the comforting illusion of infinite time to correct our course.

111
00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:17.470
<v Speaker 2>We assume that someday, somehow, our accumulated knowledge and good intentions will spontaneously translate into virtue.

112
00:08:17.470 --> 00:08:20.190
<v Speaker 1>We tell ourselves we are just in a phase of preparation.

113
00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:29.230
<v Speaker 2>But if decades pass and you arrive at 40 and the raw objective accumulation of your physical actions still tilts toward harm, negligence, or empty performance.

114
00:08:29.310 --> 00:08:33.870
<v Speaker 1>The text demands that you abandon the hope of a spontaneous, effortless transformation.

115
00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:36.230
<v Speaker 2>The habits you have built are the person you

116
00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:43.710
<v Speaker 2>You are told to prepare for the inevitable outcome of the path you have solidified through decades of repetition.

117
00:08:44.030 --> 00:08:50.430
<v Speaker 1>That hardening of habits is most catastrophic for the specific type of listener the master is addressing.

118
00:08:50.510 --> 00:08:55.470
<v Speaker 2>He is not primarily targeting the layman whose appetites simply drown out the call of conscience.

119
00:08:55.790 --> 00:09:00.190
<v Speaker 2>He is targeting the scholar, the student of religion, the philosopher.

120
00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:05.150
<v Speaker 1>This is the figure who acquires wisdom, masters the texts, and gathers worldly learning.

121
00:09:05.590 --> 00:09:10.510
<v Speaker 2>This individual imagines that the sheer possession of this knowledge will be his means of salvation.

122
00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:15.390
<v Speaker 1>All while enjoying the fame, deference, and prestige it brings him in the current life.

123
00:09:15.550 --> 00:09:18.670
<v Speaker 2>This self-deception leads to the third and heaviest warning.

124
00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:21.630
<v Speaker 1>Which is at the absolute core of the scholar's identity.

125
00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:28.030
<v Speaker 2>It states, on the day of judgment, the most severe torment will be visited upon the scholar whom Allah did not benefit through his knowledge.

126
00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:30.910
<v Speaker 2>That is, one who did not act upon what he acquired.

127
00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:34.510
<v Speaker 1>Notice exactly who is most exposed to danger here.

128
00:09:34.910 --> 00:09:36.350
<v Speaker 1>It is not the ignorant.

129
00:09:36.590 --> 00:09:38.350
<v Speaker 1>It is not the openly rebellious.

130
00:09:38.590 --> 00:09:39.870
<v Speaker 2>It is the learned soul.

131
00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:45.310
<v Speaker 2>The scholar acquires the terminology, learns the complex arguments, and masters the sciences.

132
00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:51.150
<v Speaker 1>But somewhere along that journey of accumulation, the scholar makes a fatal internal substitution.

133
00:09:51.310 --> 00:09:54.750
<v Speaker 2>He assumes that the mastery of the theory is the salvation.

134
00:09:55.630 --> 00:10:01.070
<v Speaker 2>He effectively grants himself an exemption from the physical, demanding, unseen like of the body.

135
00:10:01.150 --> 00:10:06.990
<v Speaker 1>He believes that because he understands the theory of the work so perfectly, he is no longer required to perform it.

136
00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:11.870
<v Speaker 2>The deference and prestige the scholar enjoys become a psychological shield.

137
00:10:12.550 --> 00:10:17.950
<v Speaker 2>Because others constantly look to him for answers, he assumes he has already arrived at the destination.

138
00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:23.950
<v Speaker 1>He thinks it is beneath him, or simply unnecessary, to engage in the basic physical acts of obedience.

139
00:10:24.110 --> 00:10:30.510
<v Speaker 2>He does not realize that the very knowledge he acquired and left untouched will transform into the instrument of his own prosecution.

140
00:10:30.830 --> 00:10:32.990
<v Speaker 1>It is one thing to fail out of genuine ignorance.

141
00:10:32.990 --> 00:10:41.950
<v Speaker 2>It is an entirely different order of failure to hold the map in your hands, to explain the map beautifully to a crowd of admirers, and yet refuse to take a single physical step

142
00:10:42.070 --> 00:10:43.070
<v Speaker 2>the path it outlines.

143
00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:50.510
<v Speaker 1>The danger of this unacted upon knowledge moves from a theoretical warning into a visceral vision from beyond the grave.

144
00:10:50.750 --> 00:10:57.550
<v Speaker 2>To break through the student's defenses, the master steps away from the prophetic warnings and turns to a recorded dream.

145
00:10:57.790 --> 00:10:59.870
<v Speaker 1>Forcing you to confront the ultimate accounting.

146
00:10:59.950 --> 00:11:05.630
<v Speaker 2>He recounts the dream of a great master of the inner path who shaped generations of seekers, J.

147
00:11:05.670 --> 00:11:06.110
<v Speaker 2>Unaid,

148
00:11:06.670 --> 00:11:10.270
<v Speaker 2>We are speaking of one of the most revered figures in the entire tradition.

149
00:11:10.270 --> 00:11:16.030
<v Speaker 1>A man whose public discourses, lectures, and spiritual signs define the path for countless disciples.

150
00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:22.990
<v Speaker 2>After this great master's death, a certain saint sees him in a dream and asks him a simple, direct question.

151
00:11:23.150 --> 00:11:24.590
<v Speaker 1>Tell me is your state after death?

152
00:11:24.830 --> 00:11:31.390
<v Speaker 2>The reply delivered in the dream is staggering in its brevity and its dismissal of a lifetime of public achievement.

153
00:11:31.470 --> 00:11:35.550
<v Speaker 1>He says, all discourses and spiritual signs turned out to be of no avail.

154
00:11:36.110 --> 00:11:36.470
<v Speaker 1>But

155
00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:39.550
<v Speaker 1>cycles of prayer I had performed during the night were of benefit.

156
00:11:39.630 --> 00:11:42.990
<v Speaker 2>We really need to unpack the devastation of that phrase of no avail.

157
00:11:42.990 --> 00:11:45.870
<v Speaker 1>The phrase strips away every illusion of worldly legacy.

158
00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:53.230
<v Speaker 2>His teachings, his lectures, his subtle indications, his libraries of books, none of these were condemned as evil.

159
00:11:53.550 --> 00:11:55.070
<v Speaker 2>They were simply weightless.

160
00:11:55.150 --> 00:11:59.950
<v Speaker 1>When placed on the scale that determines his eternal state, they registered as zero.

161
00:12:00.470 --> 00:12:09.950
<v Speaker 2>The public acts of teaching, the brilliant insights that moved crowds to tears, the gathering of devoted disciples, none of it tipped the scale in his favor.

162
00:12:10.190 --> 00:12:14.030
<v Speaker 1>What actually weighed on the scale, the only thing that had mass in the next world,

163
00:12:14.550 --> 00:12:18.590
<v Speaker 1>was a small, specific number of cycles of voluntary night prayer.

164
00:12:18.910 --> 00:12:25.230
<v Speaker 2>The text specifies this practice as ta e jjud, the prayer performed in the final, deepest portion of the night.

165
00:12:25.390 --> 00:12:28.750
<v Speaker 1>This is a practice defined entirely by secrecy and physical effort.

166
00:12:28.830 --> 00:12:35.870
<v Speaker 2>It requires a person to leave the warmth of their bed in the dark when absolutely no one is watching and perform physical movements of devotion.

167
00:12:35.870 --> 00:12:37.630
<v Speaker 1>It is not a conceptual exercise.

168
00:12:37.790 --> 00:12:38.590
<v Speaker 1>It is bodily.

169
00:12:38.830 --> 00:12:42.990
<v Speaker 2>It forces the ego into submission through physical exhaustion and isolation.

170
00:12:43.150 --> 00:12:44.190
<v Speaker 1>The contrast

171
00:12:44.510 --> 00:12:47.230
<v Speaker 1>in this dream forms the spine of the entire letter.

172
00:12:47.230 --> 00:12:52.190
<v Speaker 2>The smallest acted upon practice outweighed an entire lifetime of taught but untouched wisdom.

173
00:12:52.430 --> 00:13:02.430
<v Speaker 1>The physical act of standing in the dark, bowing and prostrating actions that require no advanced degree, no eloquence, and no audience to understand carried eternal weight.

174
00:13:02.510 --> 00:13:08.670
<v Speaker 2>The vast oceans of theoretical knowledge delivered masterfully to thousands carried no weight at all.

175
00:13:08.830 --> 00:13:13.870
<v Speaker 1>This dream is the master's entire thesis rendered in a single devastating image.

176
00:13:14.350 --> 00:13:18.110
<v Speaker 1>that the currency of the next world is not intellect, it is action.

177
00:13:18.590 --> 00:13:22.510
<v Speaker 2>Knowledge alone without righteous deeds will not benefit you on the day of judgment.

178
00:13:22.670 --> 00:13:29.870
<v Speaker 1>To ensure this verdict cannot be debated, misunderstood, or rationalized away, the Master delivers 2 strict parables.

179
00:13:30.790 --> 00:13:32.750
<v Speaker 2>These are not gentle, comforting stories.

180
00:13:32.990 --> 00:13:37.950
<v Speaker 2>They're engineered to violently strip away any remaining illusions the disciple might hold about his own safety.

181
00:13:38.030 --> 00:13:41.870
<v Speaker 1>The first parable places a person in the sudden, mortal danger of a jungle.

182
00:13:42.030 --> 00:13:45.110
<v Speaker 2>A person is passing through a dense jungle or a harsh desert.

183
00:13:45.230 --> 00:13:46.430
<v Speaker 2>He is heavily armed.

184
00:13:46.430 --> 00:13:51.110
<v Speaker 2>He is carrying 10 sharp swords, fine bows, and various other specialized weapons.

185
00:13:51.110 --> 00:13:53.150
<v Speaker 1>More importantly, he is not just carrying them.

186
00:13:53.470 --> 00:13:56.590
<v Speaker 1>He possesses full, perfect knowledge of the art of war.

187
00:13:56.750 --> 00:13:59.950
<v Speaker 2>He knows exactly how to wield every weapon he carries.

188
00:14:00.550 --> 00:14:03.470
<v Speaker 2>their balance, their reach, and their lethality.

189
00:14:03.790 --> 00:14:08.910
<v Speaker 1>Then suddenly a massive, terrifying lion confronts him, ready to attack.

190
00:14:09.310 --> 00:14:11.870
<v Speaker 2>The master asks a rhetorical question of the student.

191
00:14:12.190 --> 00:14:16.190
<v Speaker 2>Can that person be saved from the lion without actually using those weapons?

192
00:14:16.270 --> 00:14:18.270
<v Speaker 1>The answer is obvious and undeniable.

193
00:14:18.510 --> 00:14:19.310
<v Speaker 1>He cannot.

194
00:14:19.550 --> 00:14:22.510
<v Speaker 2>The components of this parable are exact and unforgiving.

195
00:14:22.910 --> 00:14:24.430
<v Speaker 2>The weapons are not defective.

196
00:14:24.590 --> 00:14:27.510
<v Speaker 2>The man's knowledge of warfare is not flawed or incomplete.

197
00:14:28.110 --> 00:14:31.230
<v Speaker 1>The lion is completely real and its intent is lethal.

198
00:14:31.790 --> 00:14:35.790
<v Speaker 1>The crisis rests entirely on the gap between potential and action.

199
00:14:35.870 --> 00:14:42.910
<v Speaker 2>Knowledge standing perfectly in the mind and weapons held securely in the hand cannot substitute for the actual physical swing of the arm.

200
00:14:43.150 --> 00:14:46.590
<v Speaker 1>The man can recite the precise metallurgical properties of the sword.

201
00:14:46.750 --> 00:14:48.910
<v Speaker 1>He can explain the physics of the bow's tension.

202
00:14:48.910 --> 00:14:55.750
<v Speaker 2>He can lecture the lion on the vast history of human warfare, but unless his arm physically swings the weapon, he will be torn apart.

203
00:14:55.950 --> 00:14:57.990
<v Speaker 1>The master draws the parallel directly

204
00:14:58.110 --> 00:14:58.670
<v Speaker 1>scholar.

205
00:14:58.830 --> 00:15:09.070
<v Speaker 2>If someone knows 100,000 intricate points of jurisprudence but does not act on them, that knowledge will be of absolutely no benefit when the crisis arrives.

206
00:15:10.110 --> 00:15:13.150
<v Speaker 1>Jurisprudence without action cannot save you from the lion.

207
00:15:13.310 --> 00:15:18.270
<v Speaker 2>The lion represents the ultimate, unavoidable accounting of death and judgment.

208
00:15:18.590 --> 00:15:21.550
<v Speaker 2>You cannot present your library card to the lion.

209
00:15:21.790 --> 00:15:26.110
<v Speaker 1>You cannot hand the lion a list of your credentials or a summary of your debates.

210
00:15:26.510 --> 00:15:28.030
<v Speaker 1>You must swing the sword.

211
00:15:28.270 --> 00:15:32.670
<v Speaker 2>The knowledge only becomes a shield when it is translated into the physical motion of a deed.

212
00:15:32.670 --> 00:15:39.870
<v Speaker 1>The second parable shifts the scene from the sudden, violent crisis of the jungle to the slow, creeping decline of the sick room.

213
00:15:40.190 --> 00:15:42.750
<v Speaker 2>A sick person is suffering from a fatal illness.

214
00:15:42.910 --> 00:15:45.790
<v Speaker 2>He knows exactly which medicines contain his cure.

215
00:15:45.790 --> 00:15:50.910
<v Speaker 1>He possesses flawless knowledge of their healing properties, their ingredients, and their dosages.

216
00:15:50.910 --> 00:15:54.030
<v Speaker 2>Yet he leaves the medicine sitting on the table unswallowed.

217
00:15:54.110 --> 00:15:56.110
<v Speaker 1>Will the abstract knowledge of those medicines

218
00:15:56.190 --> 00:15:57.950
<v Speaker 1>cure the physical illness in his body.

219
00:15:58.110 --> 00:15:58.590
<v Speaker 2>No.

220
00:15:59.150 --> 00:16:03.630
<v Speaker 2>Knowing the chemical composition of the cure does nothing to alter the corrupted state of the blood.

221
00:16:04.110 --> 00:16:07.630
<v Speaker 2>The body remains sick while the mind perfectly understands the cure.

222
00:16:07.710 --> 00:16:13.150
<v Speaker 1>The master then delivers the final punchline to this sequence with the brevity of a poet.

223
00:16:13.390 --> 00:16:18.910
<v Speaker 2>Even if you purchase 2,000 pounds of wine, you will not become intoxicated until you physically drink from it.

224
00:16:19.230 --> 00:16:24.670
<v Speaker 1>Look at the three distinct registers of human urgency the master has just deployed to trap the student.

225
00:16:25.310 --> 00:16:30.030
<v Speaker 2>The lion represents mortal danger, immediate, violent, undeniable.

226
00:16:30.270 --> 00:16:39.150
<v Speaker 1>The sick man represents long patience, a slow, creeping reality where the end is absolutely certain but delayed over time.

227
00:16:39.470 --> 00:16:47.470
<v Speaker 2>The man surrounded by wine represents obvious foolishness, the sheer absurdity of acquiring a substance for a specific effect and then stubbornly refusing to ingest it.

228
00:16:47.950 --> 00:16:50.670
<v Speaker 1>In all three scenarios, the diagnosis remains identical.

229
00:16:51.310 --> 00:16:54.270
<v Speaker 1>The substance to solve the problem is perfectly present.

230
00:16:54.350 --> 00:17:00.350
<v Speaker 2>The intellectual knowledge of how the substance works is complete, but the physical body has not received the substance.

231
00:17:00.590 --> 00:17:06.030
<v Speaker 1>The arm has not swung the sword, the throat has not swallowed the medicine, the lips have not tasted the wine.

232
00:17:06.270 --> 00:17:14.350
<v Speaker 2>The physical self has been granted an exemption from the process, and because of that single failure, the entire process fails completely.

233
00:17:14.830 --> 00:17:17.310
<v Speaker 2>Knowing is not a substitute for doing.

234
00:17:17.630 --> 00:17:19.070
<v Speaker 1>Moving from the parables,

235
00:17:19.550 --> 00:17:24.670
<v Speaker 1>The master locks his argument into place using the foundational texts of the tradition.

236
00:17:24.910 --> 00:17:30.350
<v Speaker 2>Specifically the verses regarding the atom's weight and the absolute necessity of striving.

237
00:17:30.590 --> 00:17:36.750
<v Speaker 1>The word used for this intense, physical, ego-crushing striving is mujehada.

238
00:17:37.230 --> 00:17:42.030
<v Speaker 2>The master quotes the undeniable verse, human beings will attain only what they have striven for.

239
00:17:42.190 --> 00:17:47.710
<v Speaker 1>The master anticipates a scholarly objection here because he knows exactly how the learned mind operates.

240
00:17:47.870 --> 00:17:55.310
<v Speaker 2>He knows the student might attempt to argue that this specific verse was abrogated by other verses emphasizing grace or predestination.

241
00:17:55.310 --> 00:17:58.430
<v Speaker 1>The master does not even bother to engage in a debate over abrogation.

242
00:17:58.590 --> 00:18:06.230
<v Speaker 2>He simply grants the objection for the sake of argument and then lines up three more verses that are universally accepted, forming an unbreakable backstop.

243
00:18:06.710 --> 00:18:10.270
<v Speaker 1>Quotes the verse as establishing the microscopic precision of the ultimate scales.

244
00:18:10.510 --> 00:18:16.350
<v Speaker 2>Whoever has done an atom's weight of good shall see it, and whoever has done an atom's way of evil shall see it.

245
00:18:16.510 --> 00:18:22.430
<v Speaker 1>He quotes the verse requiring anyone who hopes to meet their Lord to actually perform righteous deeds.

246
00:18:22.590 --> 00:18:27.550
<v Speaker 2>Then he points to the recurring, unbreakable conjunction found repeatedly throughout the text.

247
00:18:27.550 --> 00:18:34.110
<v Speaker 1>Verily, those who believe in Allah and perform righteous deeds, these two elements are named the exact same breath.

248
00:18:34.270 --> 00:18:36.350
<v Speaker 2>They are prominently welded together.

249
00:18:37.310 --> 00:18:41.710
<v Speaker 2>The master adds another verse detailing the explicit conditions of exception from ruin.

250
00:18:41.870 --> 00:18:47.790
<v Speaker 1>Except for those who repented from their sins and believed and performed righteous deeds.

251
00:18:47.870 --> 00:18:48.750
<v Speaker 1>Repentance.

252
00:18:49.750 --> 00:18:50.990
<v Speaker 2>Faith, action.

253
00:18:51.470 --> 00:18:56.030
<v Speaker 2>It is a required sequence, and action is the inescapable final step.

254
00:18:56.270 --> 00:19:02.990
<v Speaker 1>To cement this required sequence, the master brings forth 2 foundational definitions of the religion to remove any lingering doubt.

255
00:19:02.990 --> 00:19:05.310
<v Speaker 2>First, he cites the hadith of the five pillars.

256
00:19:05.550 --> 00:19:07.950
<v Speaker 2>The structure of Islam is built upon 5 things.

257
00:19:08.110 --> 00:19:13.950
<v Speaker 2>Testifying, establishing regular prayers, paying obligatory charity, fasting, and performing pilgrimage.

258
00:19:14.190 --> 00:19:15.950
<v Speaker 1>Notice the verb the prophet uses.

259
00:19:16.030 --> 00:19:16.590
<v Speaker 1>It is built.

260
00:19:16.670 --> 00:19:19.670
<v Speaker 2>Four of those five pillars are taxing, demanding,

261
00:19:20.030 --> 00:19:21.630
<v Speaker 2>physical actions of the body.

262
00:19:22.110 --> 00:19:26.030
<v Speaker 2>They require time, money, hunger, and physical exhaustion.

263
00:19:26.350 --> 00:19:32.270
<v Speaker 1>The 5th, the testimony, is the physical action of the tongue ratifying the unseen belief of the heart.

264
00:19:32.270 --> 00:19:36.190
<v Speaker 2>Then comes the hadith, defining what faith itself actually is.

265
00:19:36.590 --> 00:19:41.070
<v Speaker 2>Faith, or E-M-A-A-N, is defined in three inseparable parts.

266
00:19:41.230 --> 00:19:46.590
<v Speaker 1>To testify with the kung, to affirm with the heart, and to act upon the teachings of Islam with the limbs.

267
00:19:47.150 --> 00:19:55.070
<v Speaker 1>So tying faith inextricably to physical bodily movement seems to reduce an internal spiritual state to a mechanical checklist.

268
00:19:55.630 --> 00:19:58.590
<v Speaker 1>Belief and intent are inherently internal realities.

269
00:19:58.910 --> 00:20:06.670
<v Speaker 1>So if we insist that faith must include the action of the limbs, aren't we turning faith into a visible performance rather than an invisible truth?

270
00:20:07.110 --> 00:20:12.510
<v Speaker 1>Does this mean a paralyzed person or someone physically incapable of these specific movements possesses no faith?

271
00:20:12.670 --> 00:20:15.750
<v Speaker 2>I will just pause and let a sentence of silence pass before I answer that.

272
00:20:15.830 --> 00:20:22.550
<v Speaker 2>The tradition anticipates exactly that distinction, which is why we must look at how the hadith of the five pillars defines the architecture of belief.

273
00:20:22.590 --> 00:20:23.830
<v Speaker 1>It's A structural argument.

274
00:20:23.950 --> 00:20:26.990
<v Speaker 2>The master is not addressing a lack of physical capacity.

275
00:20:27.390 --> 00:20:35.070
<v Speaker 2>The tradition universally accommodates the sick, the frail, and the paralyzed, judging them by their intent and whatever limits they can reach.

276
00:20:35.310 --> 00:20:36.590
<v Speaker 1>He is directly addressing

277
00:20:36.670 --> 00:20:42.990
<v Speaker 1>a scholar who has total vibrant physical capacity but chooses paralysis out of arrogance.

278
00:20:43.310 --> 00:20:54.670
<v Speaker 2>He is addressing the specific individual who claims their internal reality is so pure, so elevated, and so intellectually refined that it no longer requires the crude external effort of the body.

279
00:20:54.750 --> 00:21:02.190
<v Speaker 1>To the master, an internal reality that does not push the limbs into motion when the limbs are fully capable is not a refined reality.

280
00:21:02.510 --> 00:21:03.870
<v Speaker 1>It is a dead reality.

281
00:21:03.870 --> 00:21:06.550
<v Speaker 2>Leaving out the action of the limbs when you possess the power

282
00:21:06.630 --> 00:21:09.270
<v Speaker 2>to move them doesn't just result in a weakened state of faith.

283
00:21:09.710 --> 00:21:11.710
<v Speaker 2>By definition, it ceases to be faith at all.

284
00:21:12.030 --> 00:21:17.870
<v Speaker 1>If the heart truly affirms the reality of the Creator, the body has no choice but to move in servitude.

285
00:21:17.870 --> 00:21:20.510
<v Speaker 2>The limbs are the undeniable evidence of the heart's conviction.

286
00:21:20.510 --> 00:21:24.670
<v Speaker 1>This unyielding insistence on action leads to a crucial juncture in the letter.

287
00:21:24.830 --> 00:21:31.310
<v Speaker 2>The master has driven the point so hard that he anticipates a massive theological pitfall forming in the student's mind.

288
00:21:31.470 --> 00:21:35.950
<v Speaker 1>If he pushes the absolute necessity of physical deeds this relentlessly,

289
00:21:36.590 --> 00:21:40.990
<v Speaker 1>The student might naturally conclude that salvation is a strict mathematical transaction.

290
00:21:41.310 --> 00:21:45.470
<v Speaker 2>That deeds are the literal currency that buys entry into paradise.

291
00:21:45.710 --> 00:21:51.470
<v Speaker 1>The master pauses the entire momentum of the letter to deliver a careful, essential clarification.

292
00:21:51.870 --> 00:22:00.270
<v Speaker 2>He writes, should a thought arise in your heart that I am saying a servant of God will enter paradise, not by Allah's grace, but solely by his own deeds.

293
00:22:00.670 --> 00:22:02.750
<v Speaker 2>Know that you have not understood my meaning.

294
00:22:02.910 --> 00:22:07.710
<v Speaker 1>On the contrary, a servant will enter paradise because of Allah's favor, generosity, and blessings.

295
00:22:07.790 --> 00:22:11.390
<v Speaker 2>We are looking at a profound paradox that sits at the center of the text.

296
00:22:11.470 --> 00:22:20.590
<v Speaker 1>The Master spends pages proving through parables and verses that without physical deeds you are utterly doomed, you will be eaten by the lion, you will die of the illness.

297
00:22:20.670 --> 00:22:25.150
<v Speaker 2>Then in the very next breath, he explicitly states that your deeds do not save you.

298
00:22:26.070 --> 00:22:27.070
<v Speaker 2>Only grace saves you.

299
00:22:27.390 --> 00:22:33.150
<v Speaker 1>Unpacking this requires us to look at the exact mechanism of the relationship between human action and divine grace.

300
00:22:33.310 --> 00:22:34.830
<v Speaker 2>Deeds are not the payment.

301
00:22:34.990 --> 00:22:41.310
<v Speaker 2>You cannot purchase eternal salvation with a few decades of flawed, distracted, and often self-serving human effort.

302
00:22:41.550 --> 00:22:43.550
<v Speaker 1>The math simply does not work.

303
00:22:43.950 --> 00:22:48.430
<v Speaker 1>The value of eternal paradise infinitely outweighs the value of your limited worship.

304
00:22:48.510 --> 00:22:52.830
<v Speaker 2>Grace is the door through which one must pass, but that door has a hinge.

305
00:22:53.070 --> 00:22:54.590
<v Speaker 2>The deeds are the hinge.

306
00:22:55.110 --> 00:23:03.870
<v Speaker 1>The master argues that unless a servant makes himself deserving of mercy through the physical posture of worship and obedience, he will not be in a position to receive that mercy.

307
00:23:03.950 --> 00:23:08.110
<v Speaker 2>The soul's active posture of worship prepares the ground for the grace to land.

308
00:23:08.590 --> 00:23:11.870
<v Speaker 2>Without the deeds, the ground is hard and rejects the rain.

309
00:23:12.030 --> 00:23:14.910
<v Speaker 1>The master seals this pivotal concept with a verse.

310
00:23:15.870 --> 00:23:19.070
<v Speaker 1>Verily, the mercy of Allah is close to the doers of good.

311
00:23:19.310 --> 00:23:22.830
<v Speaker 2>The mercy is near, but it is not near to everyone indiscriminately.

312
00:23:22.830 --> 00:23:25.150
<v Speaker 2>It is near to a specific category of people.

313
00:23:25.230 --> 00:23:28.350
<v Speaker 2>It is near to those who are acting, the doers of good.

314
00:23:28.590 --> 00:23:33.870
<v Speaker 1>The master then repeats his own question twice to force the dense student to finally absorb it.

315
00:23:34.110 --> 00:23:38.190
<v Speaker 1>How then will a servant enter paradise without the mercy of Allah?

316
00:23:38.430 --> 00:23:39.870
<v Speaker 2>I repeat this important point.

317
00:23:40.270 --> 00:23:42.910
<v Speaker 2>How will a servant enter paradise without?

318
00:23:43.870 --> 00:23:53.150
<v Speaker 1>If the student tries to retreat to the comforting argument that we enter on account of internal faith alone, without the need for deeds, the master blocks that exit too.

319
00:23:53.230 --> 00:23:58.990
<v Speaker 2>He warns that relying on faith alone means facing formidable, terrifying valleys of obstacles.

320
00:23:59.350 --> 00:24:06.030
<v Speaker 2>The most daunting of these obstacles is carrying one's faith safely to the very end of life, through the agonies of death.

321
00:24:06.270 --> 00:24:09.470
<v Speaker 1>Faith is not a static possession locked safely in a vault.

322
00:24:09.630 --> 00:24:12.030
<v Speaker 1>It is a journey, and the journey is hazardous.

323
00:24:12.030 --> 00:24:20.670
<v Speaker 2>Without the protective, hardening shell of righteous deeds surrounding it, the core of faith is exposed, fragile, and easily lost or corrupted before the end arrives.

324
00:24:20.830 --> 00:24:27.950
<v Speaker 1>To illustrate the exact psychological posture required to receive this grace, the master tells the story of the angel and the worshipper.

325
00:24:29.070 --> 00:24:31.390
<v Speaker 2>Functions as the inverse of Junaid's dream.

326
00:24:31.710 --> 00:24:35.190
<v Speaker 2>In the dream, the tiny physical action was the only thing that counted.

327
00:24:35.390 --> 00:24:42.510
<v Speaker 1>In this story, the worshipper is explicitly told his lifetime of action is completely worthless.

328
00:24:42.590 --> 00:24:47.390
<v Speaker 2>There was a man among the children of Israel who worshipped Allah with immense unbroken devotion.

329
00:24:47.590 --> 00:24:49.230
<v Speaker 2>An angel was sent to test him.

330
00:24:49.470 --> 00:24:54.510
<v Speaker 1>The angel arrived with a message engineered to break the man's spirit and shatter his worldview.

331
00:24:54.670 --> 00:24:58.950
<v Speaker 2>The angel announced, Allah the Holy says that you have been tormenting your

332
00:24:59.030 --> 00:25:03.790
<v Speaker 2>yourself to no purpose, for your worship is not accepted by him and you will go to hell.

333
00:25:04.190 --> 00:25:06.190
<v Speaker 1>Put yourself in the position of that worshiper.

334
00:25:06.830 --> 00:25:13.870
<v Speaker 1>You have dedicated your entire life to physical exertion, to fasting, to prayer, to isolation, to obedience.

335
00:25:13.910 --> 00:25:20.990
<v Speaker 2>An angel, an undeniable, terrifying messenger of absolute truth stands before you and tells you that the account is closed.

336
00:25:21.470 --> 00:25:23.150
<v Speaker 2>The effort is entirely rejected.

337
00:25:23.230 --> 00:25:24.590
<v Speaker 2>Your destination is hell.

338
00:25:24.830 --> 00:25:30.270
<v Speaker 1>The transaction you thought you were making, trading earthly discomfort for eternal reward, is voided in an instant.

339
00:25:30.270 --> 00:25:36.430
<v Speaker 2>The worshipper's response is the still center of the entire chapter, the ultimate example of the posture the student must achieve.

340
00:25:36.430 --> 00:25:38.590
<v Speaker 1>The worshipper does not collapse into despair.

341
00:25:38.830 --> 00:25:40.430
<v Speaker 1>He does not try to bargain with the angel.

342
00:25:40.510 --> 00:25:45.070
<v Speaker 2>He does not pull out a ledger of his good deeds and demand an audit from the heavens.

343
00:25:45.310 --> 00:25:48.430
<v Speaker 2>He simply states his singular defining duty.

344
00:25:48.550 --> 00:25:48.990
<v Speaker 1>He says,

345
00:25:49.510 --> 00:25:55.390
<v Speaker 1>Allah the Exalted created us for His worship and servitude, and our duty is to serve Him no matter what.

346
00:25:55.390 --> 00:26:00.270
<v Speaker 2>He is the owner of all things and all people, including myself, and He is the all-powerful.

347
00:26:00.750 --> 00:26:05.790
<v Speaker 1>The worshiper survives this devastating test because he is not operating on a transactional model.

348
00:26:06.270 --> 00:26:09.870
<v Speaker 1>He recognizes his fundamental, inescapable nature as a servant.

349
00:26:10.110 --> 00:26:12.510
<v Speaker 2>A servant does not dictate terms to the owner.

350
00:26:12.670 --> 00:26:14.350
<v Speaker 2>A servant does not demand payment.

351
00:26:14.670 --> 00:26:17.230
<v Speaker 2>A servant serves because that is what a servant is.

352
00:26:17.230 --> 00:26:19.070
<v Speaker 2>It is his ontological reality.

353
00:26:19.230 --> 00:26:22.110
<v Speaker 1>The worship was not a coin he was using to buy paradise.

354
00:26:22.670 --> 00:26:25.630
<v Speaker 1>The worship was the inescapable reality of his existence.

355
00:26:25.790 --> 00:26:34.430
<v Speaker 2>Because the worshiper's service became fixed, absolute, and unconditional, divorced entirely from the calculation of reward, God did not turn his face away.

356
00:26:34.590 --> 00:26:38.190
<v Speaker 1>The angel returns to the heavens and reports the man's steadfast reply.

357
00:26:38.230 --> 00:26:38.990
<v Speaker 2>Allah answers.

358
00:26:38.990 --> 00:26:45.710
<v Speaker 2>Since this servant of ours does not turn his face away from our worship and servitude, we too will not turn our face away from him.

359
00:26:45.950 --> 00:26:49.150
<v Speaker 1>The prophet's seal upon the story is the divine declaration.

360
00:26:50.510 --> 00:26:54.430
<v Speaker 1>my angels, bear witness, all of you, that I have forgiven him.

361
00:26:54.670 --> 00:26:57.950
<v Speaker 2>The dynamic revealed here is vital for you to understand.

362
00:26:58.350 --> 00:27:07.070
<v Speaker 2>The action grounded purely in obedience, totally divorced from the calculation of return, drew down the very forgiveness that Tess pretended to withhold.

363
00:27:07.230 --> 00:27:08.750
<v Speaker 1>Action is its own ground.

364
00:27:09.070 --> 00:27:12.830
<v Speaker 1>When you act out of absolute servitude, recognizing you deserve nothing,

365
00:27:13.470 --> 00:27:15.630
<v Speaker 1>Grace meets you on that exact ground.

366
00:27:15.710 --> 00:27:19.950
<v Speaker 2>This profound realization brings the master to the closing turn of the section.

367
00:27:20.270 --> 00:27:25.390
<v Speaker 2>He moves from the harsh diagnosis to the hinge of the cure, the practice of self-accounting.

368
00:27:25.710 --> 00:27:31.630
<v Speaker 1>He quotes the command, take account of yourselves before you are taken to account on the day of judgment and weigh your deeds.

369
00:27:31.950 --> 00:27:39.470
<v Speaker 2>That is, critically examine and correct the shortcomings in your deeds before your deeds are weighed and judged, and reward or punishment is decreed.

370
00:27:39.710 --> 00:27:48.270
<v Speaker 1>Establishing an inner court in this world allows you to render a verdict on yourself before the terrifying, inescapable outer court in the next world does it for you.

371
00:27:48.670 --> 00:27:55.230
<v Speaker 2>It requires A brutal, unflinching honesty about what you actually do with your limbs versus what you merely know in your head.

372
00:27:55.550 --> 00:28:01.630
<v Speaker 1>To cement the exact posture you must take in this inner court, the master lines up 3.

373
00:28:02.750 --> 00:28:06.030
<v Speaker 2>The first witness is the commander of the faithful, peace be upon him.

374
00:28:06.350 --> 00:28:11.790
<v Speaker 2>He states that a person who thinks he will reach paradise without righteous striving is hopelessly self-deluded.

375
00:28:12.030 --> 00:28:17.950
<v Speaker 1>And a person who thinks he will reach paradise through striving alone is merely exhausting himself.

376
00:28:18.110 --> 00:28:20.750
<v Speaker 2>It is the perfect articulation of the paradox.

377
00:28:21.270 --> 00:28:26.350
<v Speaker 2>You must strive with everything you have, but you must absolutely not rely on the striving to save you.

378
00:28:26.750 --> 00:28:30.110
<v Speaker 1>The second witness is an early teacher of the city of Basra, A.J.

379
00:28:30.110 --> 00:28:31.070
<v Speaker 1>San El-Baisri.

380
00:28:31.230 --> 00:28:33.950
<v Speaker 2>His statement cuts straight to the bone of human arrogance.

381
00:28:34.350 --> 00:28:38.270
<v Speaker 2>To desire paradise without good deeds is itself a sin among the sins.

382
00:28:38.510 --> 00:28:40.030
<v Speaker 1>Think about the severity of that.

383
00:28:40.190 --> 00:28:46.830
<v Speaker 1>The very desire for salvation, if it is uncoupled from the physical effort to attain it, is categorized as a transgression.

384
00:28:46.990 --> 00:28:51.870
<v Speaker 2>It is an insult to the reality of the divine court to expect the reward without lifting the sword.

385
00:28:51.950 --> 00:28:58.590
<v Speaker 1>The third witness is another intimate of God who defines the exact psychological balance required to survive this journey.

386
00:28:58.910 --> 00:29:02.990
<v Speaker 2>The reality is that one should act without admiring one's own actions.

387
00:29:03.430 --> 00:29:09.230
<v Speaker 2>But one should never abandon the performance of good deeds, even if there is a risk of self-admiration.

388
00:29:09.310 --> 00:29:10.190
<v Speaker 1>You do the work.

389
00:29:10.510 --> 00:29:13.390
<v Speaker 1>You fiercely refuse to be impressed by your own work.

390
00:29:13.790 --> 00:29:17.790
<v Speaker 1>And you never let the fear of arrogance trick you into stopping the work entirely.

391
00:29:18.030 --> 00:29:22.790
<v Speaker 2>These 3 voices lock the overarching rule into place for the student and for you.

392
00:29:23.150 --> 00:29:25.870
<v Speaker 2>Striving alone does not arrive at the destination.

393
00:29:26.190 --> 00:29:28.350
<v Speaker 2>Not striding certainly does not arrive.

394
00:29:28.430 --> 00:29:32.670
<v Speaker 1>Acting without complacency, knowing without abandoning the physical act,

395
00:29:33.150 --> 00:29:36.070
<v Speaker 1>This is the exact narrow posture required.

396
00:29:36.070 --> 00:29:37.790
<v Speaker 2>The diagnosis is complete.

397
00:29:38.190 --> 00:29:44.270
<v Speaker 2>The student has been shown the terrifying figure of the scholar of 100,000 points who stands paralyzed in the jungle.

398
00:29:44.350 --> 00:29:50.430
<v Speaker 1>He has been shown the sick man holding the bottle of a cure he will not drink, the fool sitting thirsty among the barrels of wine.

399
00:29:50.510 --> 00:29:52.910
<v Speaker 2>He has been given the final doctrinal sentence.

400
00:29:53.310 --> 00:29:57.470
<v Speaker 2>Knowledge alone, without righteous deeds, will not benefit you on the day of judgment.

401
00:29:57.790 --> 00:30:00.750
<v Speaker 1>The final image left hanging in the air is the most urgent one.

402
00:30:00.910 --> 00:30:02.190
<v Speaker 2>The jungle surrounds you.

403
00:30:02.590 --> 00:30:04.350
<v Speaker 2>The lion is coming to the trees.

404
00:30:04.350 --> 00:30:06.270
<v Speaker 2>The 10 swords are heavy in your hand.

405
00:30:06.750 --> 00:30:09.310
<v Speaker 2>The question remains, will the arms swing?

406
00:30:09.990 --> 00:30:16.910
<v Speaker 1>Which known prescription sits unswallowed in your life right now, and what smallest physical deed could you begin this week to take the cure?
